{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And enough of being the voices of Elohim and hail.</b> 'And enough' (ורב) — it means 'to deliver.' Thus Onkelos translated: וסגי קדמוהי רווחא. And the explanation of the verse is: they entreated HaShem, for it is enough that He deliver them — that there be no more the voices and the hail; and he attributed the voices to the name Elohim because those voices are thunder that shatters the heart and shakes the body, coming in great might. And thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: Why were thunderclaps created? To break the stubbornness in the heart. And he placed 'voices' before 'hail,' for so it mentioned above: ״ויהוה נתן קולות וברד״ — 'and HaShem gave voices and hail' — because the thunder came first and terrified their hearts and unsettled their minds, and afterward the hail would fall upon them and they would die. And so you find in the prayer of Moshe: ״ויחדלו הקולות והברד״ — 'and the voices and the hail ceased' — for the calamity that came first is removed first. According to this, it would have been fitting for the verse to say: ״וירא פרעה כי חדלו הקולות והברד והמטר ויוסף לחטוא״ — 'and Pharaoh saw that the voices and the hail and the rain had ceased, and he continued to sin' — and the reason it mentioned them in reversed order, to attach ״ויוסף לחטוא״ to ״the voices,״ is to indicate the stubbornness of Pharaoh's heart, for he agreed to send them out only out of fear of the voices, as he said, ״ורב מהיות קולות אלהים״ — and the proof is that as soon as the voices ceased, he continued to sin."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:19.748075759Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}